ll_write() is only called from 5 different places,
but only two of those is passing in a message header,
so casting the pointer to an ldcs_message_t and looking
for the LDCS_MSG_EXIT type is dangerous.

Not a lot of ways to handle this:

1) Demote the message to debug_printf().  That's
    dangerous and potentially very annoying.
2) Hoist the error handling up across the board
    to determine if it's worth reporting.  That's
    awkward and difficult to communicate to future
    developers.
3) Create an alternative call tree dedicated to
    ldcs_message_t and check for this message.
    Lots of work for just a one-off case.
4) Add a "no_error" or "silent_error" parameter
    to the call chain.  A modest amount of work,
    but still a lot of complication to silence
    exactly one error message.

